3,3,3,3,3,7,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,7,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the grid is made of.** Every input is a black (0) canvas with one colour drawn as a lattice of straight line segments, some horizontal and some vertical. Some segments run the full width or height of the grid, and others stop short. The line cells never change between input and output. Every 0 cell becomes either 3 or 2.\n\n**Which cells become 2.** The lines cut the black area into pockets. Look at the 2-coloured areas in the outputs:\n- Pair 1, `x5..9, y4..11`: walled by the row-3 line, column 4, column 10 and the row-12 line. The cells `(7,5..7)` left of the short row-7 segment are also 2. They belong to the same pocket, because that segment only starts at x=8.\n- Pair 1, `x11..15, y8..11`: this pocket stays 3. The row-12 line ends at x=12, so the pocket leaks downward through x13..15 and reaches the open border.\n- Pair 3: the 2-area continues below the row-15 line at `x5..6, y16..18`. That strip is walled by column 4, column 7 and the short row-19 segment, so it is closed.\n\nSo the rule is connectivity. Take the 4-connected components of background cells. A component that touches the grid border is open and becomes 3. A component that is completely walled in by line cells is enclosed and becomes 2. This matches the 2 and 3 regions in all three outputs.\n\n**Applying it to the test.** The test grid has colour 7 lines. The outside region and every pocket that leaks to the border through a gap become 3. Pockets fully closed by 7-segments become 2, for example the cells between columns 5 and 11 and rows 4 and 9.\n\n**Construction stages.**\n1. Recolour every open, border-connected background component to 3.\n2. Fill every enclosed component with 2. This is the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    comps = components(g == bg)\n    enclosed, open_ = [], []\n    for c in comps:\n        if any(y == 0 or x == 0 or y == H - 1 or x == W - 1 for y, x in c):\n            open_.append(c)\n        else:\n            enclosed.append(c)\n    for c in open_:\n        for y, x in c:\n            out[y, x] = 3\n    emit(out, \"Background regions connected to the grid border (open) become 3.\")\n    for c in enclosed:\n        for y, x in c:\n            out[y, x] = 2\n    emit(out, \"Background pockets fully enclosed by the lines are filled with 2.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"So the rule is connectivity."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Take the 4-connected components of background cells."}],"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Recolour every open, border-connected background component to 3."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"A component that is completely walled in by line cells is enclosed and becomes 2."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Fill every enclosed component with 2."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Background pockets fully enclosed by the lines are filled with 2."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"9d39a04937c5a972acc1f126898bd654be288d124f58f4a9cb47fea51294a715","task_sha256":"a590b0b6031401f908f788189f8fa733dc6028f9d7453c952a7b8f41e5202964","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
